WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
And ai just built a realistic space combat game you can play in your browser right now. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 175. A researcher named Ethan Mollick asked an ai system called Fable or Opus to build a full two-dimensional starship battle. Two-dimensional just means you look down on the fight like a board game, not from inside a cockpit. He wanted the ships to obey real physics, not the swooping movie trick where a craft stopped.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:36.040
on a dime. Claude, a chatbot you may already use for writing, helped make the whole thing.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:41.560
In the finished browser game, every ship has to watch fuel, heat, and which way it turns.

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:48.040
Nothing in space eases off the gas for you. Picture sliding a car across a frozen parking lot.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:53.780
Once you start moving, you keep going until you spin the engine around and burn fuel to slow down.

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:59.560
The game tracks that remaining shove with something called Delta V, which is just how much push

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:04.500
push your engines can still give. Think of it like a gas tank for motion, except you never

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:09.600
get a refill in the middle of a fight. There is also a heat system. After a battle,

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:14.360
the ship has to stretch out radiators to cool off. The way you might stick your arms out

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:19.880
after running. If you hide those radiators during an attack, you stay safer, because they are

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:24.520
easy to hit when they are hanging out. And if you break toward an enemy, you show the

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:28.140
weaker side of your armor, like turning your back in dodge ball to stop.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:33.380
That is the kind of tactic strategy game fans and space story readers can feel in their

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:33.800
hands.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:39.480
It turns orbital mechanics, the real rules of how things move in space, into something

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:41.960
you can poke at instead of only reading in class.

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:46.180
You can try the exact game on the orbital declaration website.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:48.760
Click any ship on the left to pick it.

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:51.680
Right click an enemy to set an intercept course.

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:57.040
Then tap the order buttons on the right and watch how each move spends fuel and redraws

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:57.420
the path.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:02.460
A navigator line at the top tells you what the ship is doing now and what it will do

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:02.800
next.

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:09.380
The plotted root uses bright lines for burns, dashed lines for turns or coasts, and blue

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:10.600
gray lines for braking.

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:16.400
It is like a GPS preview, except every glowing stretch is fuel you will never get back.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:23.380
The orders you can give are intercept, keep range, match speed, hold, retreat, and manual.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:29.080
Each one shows its Delta V cost before you confirm, so you see the price tag first.

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:35.400
For weapons, press W to set them free, and press L to fire a salvo of two shots.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:41.360
Hit space to pause or press 1-4 to speed up time when you are not deciding anything.

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:45.700
Damage control lets you send repair parties to the parts that got hit.

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:52.720
it. Repairs stop if the ship pulls harder than 1.2G, which means more than 1.2 times the

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:58.840
gravity you feel standing on Earth. Crew can get hurt above 3G. Throw a ball on Earth

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:04.300
and it comes back down because gravity and air steal its speed. In space there is almost

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:10.400
no air and gravity is tiny so a moving ship just keeps going in a straight line until something

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:13.580
shoves it again. Now think about your phone battery.

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:19.120
Every time you open an app, the number drops, and you cannot wish that energy back without

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:19.760
plugging in.

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:22.060
Delta V works the same way.

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:27.380
It is the total push still sitting in the engines, and every burn or turn spends some

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:27.820
forever.

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:32.120
That is why you do not chase a target like a kid sprinting after a bus.

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:37.800
You plan ahead and match speed, more like jogging alongside a friend until you can hop on.

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:41.700
Most space shooters let you tap the brakes whenever you want.

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:47.520
This one does not because the math is quietly enforcing real orbital rules under every click.

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:50.720
After you give an order, the path redraws.

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:53.840
Coasting shows up as the cheap, dashed stretch.

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:56.780
Burns show up as the expensive, bright ones.

00:03:57.340 --> 00:04:01.940
That is the moment space travel stops feeling like a dogfight and starts feeling like careful

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:04.580
planning with long, quiet slides in between.

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:09.120
Once those lines make sense, you are not memorizing a textbook sentence.

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:11.220
You are watching the rule happen.

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:16.480
While that game is teaching physics with a joystick, a company called Rabbit built an

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:20.240
agent named OS3 that tries to run your computer for you.

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:26.900
An agent is an AI that does not only chat, it takes actions like a helpful person sitting

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:27.600
at your desk.

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:33.800
OS3 lives in the cloud, meaning it runs on far away computers, then reaches into Windows,

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:38.420
Mac, or Linux, the three common kinds of computers people actually use.

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:43.120
It can open programs, click buttons, and walk through several steps in a row.

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:48.580
You do not need Rabbids' old handheld gadget, the R1, which is a bit like streaming

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:51.800
music on any phone instead of carrying a special player.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:57.120
If you have ever wished the boring clicks would just do themselves, this is aimed at you.

00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:02.840
Go to the Rabbit website, sign up for the free tier if it is available, and ask the agent

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:08.200
to open your browser, search a homework topic, and summarize the first result in three bullet

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:13.520
points, watch whether it really clicks around or whether it gets stuck, because that gap

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:15.220
is the whole point of trying it.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:21.700
Andthropic, the company that makes Claude, also released Claude Opus 5.5 for everyday

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:22.140
use.

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:28.040
It is a stronger version of their top model, and a model here just means the trained AI brain

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:28.940
doing the talking.

00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:33.220
This one is built for longer conversations and more careful answers.

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:38.000
It is meant for people who want sharper reasoning without paying big company rates.

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:43.480
If you are writing an essay or a story, that longer memory is like a tutor who read your

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:45.580
whole draft, not just the last sentence.

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:51.120
Head to Claude's website, start a new chat, and paste a paragraph from your last school

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:51.620
assignment.

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:57.820
Ask it to rewrite that paragraph in a friendlier tone, then send a follow up like, make it shorter

00:05:57.820 --> 00:06:02.740
or keep my voice, notice whether the second answer still remembers the first one.

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:06.860
That follow-up test is the experiment, and it takes about a minute.

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:13.420
Apple's latest watch update adds audio intelligence features, which means the watch tries to understand

00:06:13.420 --> 00:06:16.180
sounds around you and respond in new ways.

00:06:16.780 --> 00:06:22.360
It is another step toward a watch that feels like an always-on helper, not only a fitness tracker.

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:27.800
Picture extra ears on your wrist that try to make sense of the room, not just count your

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:33.320
steps. And Andreessen Horowitz, a venture firm that funds new companies, is starting

00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:39.220
a freestyle academy for future startup builders. A startup is a young company still figuring

00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:46.260
itself out. Partners include Google, Meta, and OpenAI, the company behind Chat GPT. There

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:52.040
is no homework. The goal is to show students how startups actually work, and to connect them

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:57.380
with real companies, more like a backstage tour than a textbook chapter. That's it for

